Revision history for Unblock-HTTP2

0.11  2026-10-10
    - Add optional attached-transport integration for Client and Server.
      Frameworks provide unblock_send(), unblock_finish(), unblock_abort().
      Output is delivered automatically without manual output pumping.
    - Add input_eof(), transport_error(), and resume_output().
    - Preserve HTTP/2 multiplexing, stream resets, and output backpressure.
    - Support named fields in request(), respond(), and send_informational()
      while keeping canonical Uniform::HTTP messages supported.
    - Add Integration POD, a cookbook, Linux::Event, IO::Async and AnyEvent
      examples, and regression tests for host congestion, deferred responses,
      control frames, EOF, callback reentrancy, and fatal transport failures.
    - Treat fatal application control callback errors as transport aborts,
      distinct from explicit graceful close().
    - Exercise the IO::Async and AnyEvent client/server examples in Linux CI.
    - Update the public module SYNOPSIS sections to lead with the new
      attached transport and named-field API.
    - Preserve NativeABI version 1 without changing the public C header.

0.10  2026-10-04
    - Make a deliberate breaking API harmonization pass across Unblock::HTTP2.
    - Rename Transaction->inform() to Transaction->send_informational().
      No compatibility alias is provided.
    - Add Transaction->is_error() so the common transaction lifecycle
      vocabulary includes state(), error(), is_complete(), is_cancelled(),
      is_error(), and is_terminal().
    - Normalize NativeABI discovery with definition(), native_include_dir(),
      header_path(), and c_header().
    - Install the public HTTP/2 native ABI header and report struct_size in the
      Perl discovery definition.
    - Build the XS native transport implementation against that same public
      header so the installed declaration and compiled layout stay in sync.
    - Review README, public POD, and docs for the 0.10 API, including callback
      signatures, NativeABI discovery, and an explicit upgrade note.
    - Add a staged-install regression test that builds the generated
      distribution, loads NativeABI from the installed copy, verifies the
      installed header and discovery values, and compiles an external C
      consumer against that header.
    - Jump from 0.04 to 0.10 to make the intentional public API break obvious.

0.04  2026-10-04
    - Add Unblock::HTTP2::NativeABI for XS-backed transports.
    - Accept borrowed native input buffers directly on both Client and Server,
      avoiding the transport-side Perl byte-scalar materialization.
    - Add native output draining so libnghttp2 output can be copied directly
      into a transport-owned native queue without an intermediate Perl string.
    - Support native output pause-after-accept semantics for transport
      backpressure while keeping the portable input()/output() API unchanged.
    - Require Uniform::HTTP 0.06 for its header-only native C ABI.
    - Inspect exact canonical outgoing Request and Response objects directly
      from XS without allocating a Perl FastPath view.
    - Keep received initial HTTP/2 header fields native through validation and
      construct the final canonical Uniform object directly from native spans.
    - Remove per-field Perl receive callbacks for initial headers and trailers;
      trailers now cross the XS boundary once per completed header block.
    - Keep Uniform subclasses and adapters on the portable Perl contract.
    - Preserve HTTP/2 validation, header-list limits, informational responses,
      Extended CONNECT, trailers, and lifecycle semantics on the native path.
    - In the seven-sample CI comparison against the 0.03 baseline, median
      canonical submit rate improved 73.5% and full in-memory loopback improved
      173.6%.
    - Add a diagnostic native-transport benchmark. The native transport ABI is
      intended to remove Perl buffer materialization at an XS transport
      boundary; an in-process peer bridge is not used as a throughput claim.

0.03  2026-10-04
    - Rename the public per-request object from Stream to Transaction so
      Unblock::HTTP1, Unblock::HTTP2, and Unblock::HTTP3 share the same
      application-facing concept.
    - Rename the related public API: id() becomes stream_id(), stream_count()
      becomes transaction_count(), stream_for_id() becomes
      transaction_for_stream_id(), can_open_stream() becomes
      can_open_transaction(), and max_active_streams becomes
      max_active_transactions.
    - Do not provide compatibility aliases for the old Stream API. This is an
      intentional early-version API cleanup.
    - Keep HTTP/2 stream terminology for protocol-level concepts such as stream
      IDs, flow control, RST_STREAM, GOAWAY boundaries, and SETTINGS.

0.02  2026-10-04
    - Require Uniform::HTTP 0.05 and use its FastPath ABI for exact canonical
      Request and Response objects.
    - Map canonical outgoing headers directly to nghttp2_nv in XS, avoiding
      per-field Perl method dispatch and intermediate HTTP/2 header arrays.
    - Lowercase canonical field names and enforce HTTP/2 field restrictions in
      the native fast path while preserving the existing portable fallback.
    - Build received canonical Request and Response objects through trusted
      FastPath construction after Unblock has validated their HTTP/2 semantics.
    - Keep subclasses and framework adapters on the normal Uniform contract.
    - Add repeatable FastPath performance comparison tooling under xt/ and CI.
      The small-message benchmark shows large gains in native submission and
      full in-memory request/response throughput.

0.01  2026-10-04
    - Initial release of a standalone, event-loop and operating-system neutral
      HTTP/2 protocol engine.
    - Use Uniform::HTTP 0.04 Request and Response objects directly.
    - Provide byte-in/byte-out Client and Server engines with multiplexed Stream
      objects and no socket, TLS, or event-loop ownership.
    - Use a small private XS binding to libnghttp2 through Alien::nghttp2.
    - Support buffered and streaming request and response bodies, cooperative
      backpressure, trailers, informational responses, and half-close state.
    - Support ordinary CONNECT and generic Extended CONNECT.
    - Support SETTINGS inspection and updates, PING, GOAWAY, graceful draining,
      stream resets, standard HTTP/2 error codes, and preserved reset details.
    - Support application-controlled receive flow control while keeping
      connection-level credit independent from slow streams.
    - Support RFC 9218 extensible priorities and deliberately omit the
      deprecated RFC 7540 dependency-tree priority API.
    - Advertise SETTINGS_ENABLE_PUSH = 0; Server Push is not exposed.
    - Preserve fatal session errors, invalid-frame observations, unknown
      extension-frame safety, and GOAWAY details for higher-layer policy.
    - Add in-memory protocol tests, packaged-distribution distcheck/disttest,
      and bidirectional interoperability tests against stock nghttp2 tools.
    - Test Perl 5.16 on Linux, current Perl on Linux and macOS, and Strawberry
      Perl on Windows.
